From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: NANO:Critiques of Drexler

Andrew Hall:
> Before I sit down and do some calculations and grind through this with
> the effort needed to confirm or deny, I would like to see some expert
> criticism of Drexler. 

I'd like to see this, too.  I haven't even seen any reviews of
_Nanosystems_ in a scientific journal; nor have I seen any 
in-depth critique of _Nanosystems_ on the net or off of it.  As smart
as Drexler is, a book that crosses such a large number of
disciplines with such depth must have at least a few nits that critics
can pick, and _Engines_-era Drexler did not lack for vocal critics.
Drexler's fame and citation habits generated quite a bit of resentment
with at least a few research scientists : the _Artificial Life I_ proceedings
feature an article on the history of "nanotechnology" that covers
everything from Heinlein's Waldo to Feynman to Ettinger to sub-micron 
lithography, but dismisses Drexler with ill-concealed animosity.

Given the claims in _Nanosystems_ about Mhz-frequency robotics, 
eutactic diamond chemistry, self-rep machines, etc., the vocal
critic of his previous efforts, and the lack of commercial investment, 
there is a startling lack of scientific critique.

Is the book so cross-discipline that every specialist feels it's not
their place to comment?  Are the claims considered so wild that practically 
all scientists dismiss them without even reading the book?  Is
_Nanosystems_ so perfect that no one can find flaws?  I was expecting 
a firestorm by now, but instead we have dead silence.  Yet Powell's 
(our huge Portland bookstore) features _Nanosystems_ prominently in the 
chemistry section, and they say it's been selling well, so I can't 
imagine that so few chemists have been exposed to it.

