                       THE IOWA ELECTRONIC MARKETS

WHAT ARE THE IOWA ELECTRONIC MARKETS AND WHY DO THEY EXIST?
     The Iowa Electronic Markets are a collection of computerized exchanges in
which traders buy and sell financial contracts.  The contracts traded in each
market promise a liquidation value which is to be determined by the value of
certain fundamentals on a pre-specified date.  Currently two exchanges are
operating, the Iowa Earnings Market and the Iowa Economic Indicators Market,
each involving contracts based on a set of related fundamentals.  These two
exchanges and the contracts traded in them are described below.
     The Iowa Electronic Markets are operated for educational and research
purposes.  For the exchange Governors and Directors, it is a field experiment
designed to study the predictive power of markets, the behavior of traders,
and the relationship between information and prices.  Traders are provided the
opportunity to learn about financial markets and the impact of information on
prices through direct participation.  With the opportunity to make or lose
money, participants are also motivated to learn more about the fundamentals
in which they are trading.

WHAT FINANCIAL CONTRACTS ARE TRADED ON THE IOWA EARNINGS MARKET?
     The fundamentals on which Iowa Earnings Market contracts are based are
specific accounting statistics for selected companies.  The contracts
initially offered will be based on the earnings per share (EPS) of five
companies -- three in the airline industry (American, Delta and United) and
two in the computer industry (Apple and IBM).  Each company will have a
separate set of contracts for reported EPS in the first and second quarters
of 1993.  As an example, the market in IBM's first quarter EPS is comprised
of two contracts, with contract codes and liquidation values as follows:
      IBM1u  -  $0.20 + 40% of IBM's EPS for the quarter ending 03/31/93
      IBM1d  -  $0.80 - 40% of IBM's EPS for the quarter ending 03/31/93

WHAT ARE THE FUNDAMENTALS OF IOWA ECONOMIC INDICATORS MARKET CONTRACTS?
     The Iowa Economic Indicators Market offers contracts based on selected
economic statistics.  Initially, there will be two types of contracts: 
inflation rate contracts whose liquidation values will be determined by the
monthly change in the Consumer Price Index (CPI) and currency contracts in the
Mexican peso whose liquidation values will be determined by the end-of-month
exchange rate between the dollar and the Mexican peso.  For example, there are
two contracts based on the end of February exchange rate between the Mexican
Peso and the U.S. Dollar.  The code for each contract and their liquidation
values are as follows:
      MP2u:  $4.00 x (2/26/93 peso per dollar exchange rate) - $12.00
      MP2d:  $13.00 - $4.00 x (2/26/93 peso per dollar exchange rate)

WHO CAN PARTICIPATE IN THE IEM?
     Invitations to participate in the IEM are offered to members -- students,
faculty and staff -- of academic communities across the United States and
around the globe.  Prospective traders must apply to either the Iowa Earnings
Market or the Iowa Economic Indicators Market and deposit some amount of money
with the IEM administrator.

HOW MUCH CAN I INVEST IN THE IOWA ELECTRONIC MARKETS?
     The minimum investment in an Iowa Electronic Markets account is $5.00 and the
maximum is $500.  The amount you invest will be posted to your cash account. 
Funds from this account can then be used to buy individual contracts from
other traders or unit portfolios from the Iowa Electronic Markets.  If they
so choose, traders who register with the Iowa Economic Indicators Market may
also have access to the Iowa Earnings Market and vice versa.

HOW CAN I MAKE MONEY BY PARTICIPATING?
     As in other financial markets, you make money by "buying low and selling
high."  Contracts have liquidation values that depend on the expiration date
value of their underlying fundamental -- IBM1u and IBM1d liquidation values
will be determined by the first quarter 1993 EPS of IBM stock, for example. 
You will earn profits on any sale of a contract at a price higher than its
liquidation value and on any purchase of a contract at a price lower than its
liquidation value.
    
WHY DO I NEED A CASH ACCOUNT?
     When you buy an individual contract or a unit portfolio you must have the cash
to pay for that purchase.  Your cash account provides you with the necessary
liquidity -- purchases of contracts are charged to it; sales of contracts are
credited to it.  Further, as markets close the liquidation values you earn on
contracts in those markets will be credited to your cash account. 
Arrangements can be made with the IEM administrator for the withdraw of funds
from your cash account.

WHAT ARE "UNIT PORTFOLIOS"?
     Unit portfolios are the means by which contracts are issued and placed in
circulation.  For the February Mexican Peso Market a unit portfolio consists
of one MP2u contract and one MP2d contract.  For the IBM - First Quarter
Earnings Market a unit portfolio consists of one IBM1u contract and one IBM1d
contract.  The price charged for each unit portfolio is $1.00.  After a unit
portfolio is purchased from the Iowa Electronic Markets, the contracts in it
may be traded individually with other participants in the market.  Unit
portfolios may be purchased at any time, and they may also be redeemed at any
time for the same price of $1.00.
     The specification of unit portfolios and the calculation of liquidation 
values guarantee that the liquidation value of the contracts comprising a unit
portfolio is exactly $1.00, the same as the issue price of that portfolio. 
Thus the Iowa Electronic Markets neither makes nor loses money through the
issuance of unit portfolios; all amounts paid for them are distributed to
traders in the form of liquidation values.

WHEN DOES THE IEM OPEN?
     The IEM will open the Iowa Earnings Market and the Iowa Economic 
Indicators Market for trading at 9:00 a.m. CST, Thursday, January 14, 1993.  
Other markets may open and new contracts may be offered at later dates at the
discretion of the Governors of the IEM.  Trading in individual contracts will
cease as of the pre-specified liquidation date of each contract.  No closing
dates for individual markets or the IEM itself have been set, though no market
will close until all contracts offered on it have expired.  Traders can open
accounts or add cash to existing accounts at any time.  Individual traders may
close or withdraw funds from their accounts on or about the fifteenth day of
any month or on other dates mutually agreed to with the market administrators.

ARE THERE ANY COMMISSION OR ENROLLMENT FEES?
     The Iowa Electronic Markets are unique among existing financial markets in
that no commissions or other fees are charged to traders.  The only payments
you make to the Iowa Electronic Markets are the amounts you choose to invest
in the market.  The total of all invested funds will be redistributed to
traders in the form of liquidation values or cash balance refunds.  The Iowa
Electronic Markets are operated as educational and research laboratories and
all administrative costs are paid from educational and research funds.

HOW DO I MAKE TRADES?
     The Iowa Electronic Markets are fully computerized markets with no brokers
or other middlemen.  Registered traders log into their market accounts from 
their own computer terminals to check market prices or account information and 
to extend offers to buy or sell.  Cost free access is available from virtually
any personal computer or terminal attached to a network at any college or
university in the country.  Telephone/modem access is also available, though
traders must bear the cost of telephone charges.  The market remains open 24
hours a day.

HOW DO I USE MY COMPUTER TO ACCESS THE IEM?
     If your communications program or TCP\IP telnet software has communication
parameters, set them to:  Data bits--8; Parity--none; Stop bits--1.  The
preferred terminal emulation modes are Dec VT-100, 200 or 220, though other
modes are also supported.  To access the IEM via Telnet, enter one of the
following commands from your computer:
           telnet iem.biz.uiowa.edu      or      telnet 128.255.44.2.
If you do not have direct Telnet access, make a modem connection to the
University of Iowa computer system by dialing 319-335-6200 and then issue one
of the above Telnet commands.  Upon successfully connecting to the market, you
should find everything quite transparent.  If not write, e-mail or call us for
more information.

CAN I FIND THE CURRENT MARKET QUOTES WITHOUT ENROLLING?
     Yes, information on market prices is available to the general public using
the same market access procedures as those used by traders.  In addition, a
"practice" market is available for use by prospective traders to familiarize
themselves with how the market works.

