Date: Fri, 25 Jun 93 02:27:00 -0700
From: p199-ac@garnet.berkeley.edu (Zak Zaidman)
Subject: Aesthetics

(The network's down again.  Seems to do that every night at 2 p.m. PDT.  So,
I, Tim Starr, am once again sending this from an account besides my own.)

It's possible for there to be an extropian aesthetics, just as it's possible
for an Objectivist one, but there isn't much of either, yet.

It's a mistake to place aesthetics outside philosophy, but that doesn't
mean that it's a very well-developed part of philosophy.  Rand was mistaken
when she said that epistemology was the least-developed part; aesthetics
is, despite her sometimes successful efforts to improve this state of
affairs.

Art can "fit" a philosophy in at least two ways.  It can express it, and
it can measure up to its standards well.  For example, Atlas Shrugged
does a good job of expressing Objectivism, but it does a poor job of
measuring up to the Objectivist standard by which art is to be measured:
the glorification of man.  Similarly, art can express extropianism, but
no extropian standard for art has emerged.

Not all art that expresses a philosophy is enjoyable, either.  Some is -
I keep listening to "Tomorrow's World" by Joe Jackson and it's good!  I
really must post the lyrics.  But some art that expresses philosophy,
even correct philosophy, is no fun at all.

I will say that some of the most beautiful art I've ever seen has been
pointed out to me by Aristos, an Objectivist-inspired art journal.  However,
they've had to go beyond Rand to do so.  Also, Aristos' critical intro
to Objectivist aesthetics is the best exposition and critique of Rand's
views on art that I've ever seen.

Tim Starr

