Date: Fri, 25 Jun 93 12:05:39 BST
From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: AIT: Defined only for infinite strings?

>A poster on sci.crypt (I think) yesterday stated that AIT applied only
>to infinite strings.  Any finite string, he argued, could be shown to
>have a near-zero algorithmic information content by choosing an appropriate
>Turing machine to calculate it (in effect, packing the information for that
>particular string into the design of the machine).  Therefore, algorithmic
>information content is not meaningful for finite strings, he said.
>
>Hal

First, a disclaimer: All I've read of Chaitin's work is his papers in JACM
in the mid-seventies, not his more recent stuff. (I haven't read his more
recent stuff because from what I've heard of it it seems to me to be just a
recycling of what he did back then.) I'm assuming from the context of the
thread that the definition of a.i.c. is this, or something similar: choose
a universal Turing machine; then the a.i.c. of any string is the length of
the shortest input to the machine which will cause it to produce that
string as output and stop.

What the poster says is rather like saying that the longitude of a single
point on the Earth's surface is not well-defined, because one can always
choose that point as the origin of the longitude system.

Algorithmic information content is indeed not meaningful for a single
finite string, for the reason that the poster said, and that applies even
to any finite set of finite strings. It is, however, meaningful for all but
at most a finite number of finite strings.  Which finite strings are the
exceptions is determined by the choice of universal Turing machine.

Cf. computational complexity: a problem such as satisfaction of
propositional formulas is NP-complete, but each instance is computationally
trivial -- the answer is either yes or no.  Any algorithm for solving the
whole family of problems can have a finite number of particular instances
built in, which it can recognise and print out the answers to immediately.
Asymptotically, however, it must take a very long time for large enough
problems.  Note that infinite problems don't enter into the matter, and
while it is possible to talk about the a.i.c. of an infinite string, a.i.c.
is meaningful for finite strings, and it is with finite strings that
Chaitin's main results are concerned.

Just to complicate the matter more, even if we ignore the possible
exceptional strings built-in to the Turing machine, a.i.c. is still not
precisely determined on the other strings, because it will still vary with
the choice of universal Turing machine, and the type of Turing machine. 
There is nothing sacred about Turing's precise definition of the eponymous
machines. One may use an alphabet of any finite size, any finite number of
infinite tapes, any finite number of read/write heads, counters and other
extensions. This will change the a.i.c. of all but a finite number of
strings by only a small amount, relative to the asymptotic behaviour --
perhaps by a linear factor, perhaps more, I'm not sure. What matters is the
asymptotic behaviour which remains after ignoring such factors. The precise
value of a.i.c. for a particular string and a particular type of Turing
machine is not important, and by one of Chaitin's results is almost always
not even computable. I doubt if there is any possibility of computing, say,
the a.i.c. of a Boeing 747, or of comparing it with that of the Stealth
fighter.

I've also seen a couple of (rather lukewarm or unfavourable) reviews of his
work.  Readers may care to look up the following:

%A Lambalgen, Michiel van
%T Algorithmic information theory
%J J. Symbolic Logic
%V 54
%N 4
%P 1389-1400
%D 1989 Dec

The abstract reads: "We present a critical discussion of the claim that
algorithmic information theory sheds new light on Godel's first
incompleteness theorem."  It is strongly critical of Chaitin's claims for
the significance of his work.

A few soundbites:

"We discuss the alleged discovery of 'randomness in mathematics'."

"To counter these grand claims, we first give a very short proof of
Chaitin's result using general nonsense only (thereby making the need for
an explicit description of the 900,000 character 17,000 variable universal
exponential Diophantine equation, deemed to be necessary in [Chaitin's
book] slightly less pressing). While this alone would lead one to suspect
that the result is not deep, it is also argued that Chaitin's association
of randomness in nature with the mathematical phenomenon loosely described
above is rather unfortunate."

"Alleged"!  "Slightly less pressing"!  "Unfortunate"!  This is considered
strong stuff on this side of the pond. :-)

