Date: Fri, 25 Jun 93 10:45:32 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: AIT: Defined only for infinite strings?

>A poster on sci.crypt (I think) yesterday stated that AIT applied only
>to infinite strings.  Any finite string, he argued, could be shown to
>have a near-zero algorithmic information content by choosing an appropriate
>Turing machine to calculate it (in effect, packing the information for that
>particular string into the design of the machine).  Therefore, algorithmic
>information content is not meaningful for finite strings, he said.
>
>Hal

I haven't seen this particular post, but it is not true as stated here.
Much of the work on AIT deals explicity with finite, even fairly short,
strings.

Let's take a concrete example: the 32-bit binary strings.

There are of course precisely 2^32 = 4,294,967,296 of them.

Some of them are describable in very concise terms:
- "all 0s", "all 1s", "1010....repeated", and so on

Some apparently random-looking strings are even highly compressible if a
"key" (same as some particular Turing machine) is known. For example, the
apparently random-looking string "1011010001110010101010001010001" may be
discovered--by a person or program that is clever enough--to be the binary
expansion of, say, "666," which is a "special" number to some.

(I apologize if this example has made things more confusing. My point being
that any number can look "special" to the right person, to the right Turing
machine/program.)

But of all the 32-bit strings, "most" are not compressible to much less
than the length. No matter which Turing machine (program) is picked, at
most 4 of the strings can be compressed to 2 bits, 8 to 3 bits, 16 to 4
bits, and so on. The pigeonhole principle: too many strings to fit into the
smaller space of short-bitlength strings.

We can compress English strings somewhat by exploiting the redundancy, the
fact that the entropy per character of English is only about 2.3 bits per
character, instead of the nearly 6 bits per character one would otherwise
expect (2^6 = 64, enough for 26 characters, and caps, and numbers,
punctuation, etc.).

It is true that any _particular_ string can be made to look "simple." This
is the encryption problem. In fact, strings which _appear_ to be
incompressible but which actually are, given the right key, are called
"cryptoregular."

Finding the key (a specific Turing machine, if you wish to use that
language) that converts the apparently-random-looking string
"1010010100001101010001010" into "Buy IBM" is the classic cryptanalysis
problem. If the system uses a cipher, there is some hope that the
decryption is possible. If the sytems uses a "one-time pad," there is no
hope.

(So I can, in theory, convert any particular random string into a nice
simple "All 1s" string by XORing the random string with the suitable
complement string...my "special Turing machine.")

Also, the "undecideable" aspect is that it is generally undecideable
whether one has achieved maximum compression, not whether one has gotten
any compression at all. To know that no better compression exists, in
general, would be to know the solution to the halting problem.

Practically speaking, it means that most strings cannot be compressed
much--a fact known for several decades, and well familiar to Shannon,
Huffman, Lempel, Ziv, and other "classical information theory"
practitioners.


-Tim

