Subject: AIT VirtSem: "Algorithmic Information and Evolution"

Hal Finney writes:

>I appreciate Tim scanning in Chaitin's article, but I have to say that
>I really don't follow the proposed connection between AIT and evolution.
>
>Chaitin's Omega analogy shows that if you compute for an infinite amount
>of time you can generate infinite complexity.  But his example doesn't
>relate at all to the fundamental idea of evolution, the role of natural
>selection applied to self-replicating systems.  There is no assurance
>whatsoever that evolution will lead to infinite complexity.  We might
>hope that it will, but I don't see how Chaitin's argument sheds any light.

There is no statement, though, that any living organism will ever come
"close" to "infinite complexity." Chaitin argues somewhat elliptically that
evolution is making a lot of computations (the Turing machine being the
survival, the input being a genome, the outcome then is the "acceptance" of
the input....all loosely speaking, of course).

In GA and GP systems (genetic algorithms, genetic programming), string
complexity does in fact increase. One can visualize some particular
sequence like this:

Initial String:                 11111111111111111......1111111
Later (a few mutations):        11110111110111111......1111111
After a crossover:              11110111110111111......0001010
Later (more mutations, cross.): 01100110101110110......1010001

(The initial string could have been picked by tossing coins, so I'm not
implying that the initiall string is highly regular, such as all 1s or all
0s, and that it then gets more "random" with time. What I'm implying is
that the process of evolution (mutations, crossovers) alters the strings)

Are the later strings "compressible," that is, describable in many fewer
bits? I suspect not, except for compressing-out certain artifacts of
evolution (like introns). But this is an open question, as I'm sure Chaitin
would agree. It makes sense to look at this.


>
>Chaitin also points out that biological evolution can be thought of as
>having done a lot of computation.  Perhaps so, but then, so can the planets
>in their orbits around the sun, as well as the waves striking the beach.
>Again, there is no guarantee that the computation is useful or productive
>in terms of generating new complexity.

Not at all the same sense. Dawkins has made similar points, in "The Blind
Watchmaker," about the computations done by organisms and the evolution of
complexity.

I think it's not really doubted by many that evolution has produced new
complexity, in a way that the orbits of planets around the sun have not.

Some processes have, as with geology (all that folding and transformation).
Chaitin is not making a mystical point that biological evolution is unique
in creating complexity.


>Chaitin warns that his point is abstract, and perhaps I am being too
>literal in trying to see an analogy between AIT and evolution.
>
>The one place I can see a possible connection between AIT and evolution
>would be by analogy to the AIT result that a formal system can't prove
>theorems more complex than the size of its axioms.  Perhaps this could
>be taken to suggest that an evolutionary process is similarly limited
>in terms of the complexity that it can produce?

I doubt this, as the "axioms" of life/biology  are ill-defined (if present
at all). I see no limits whatsoever on overall complexity.

I thank Hal for making his points.

-Tim
