Date: Fri, 25 Jun 93 14:47:13 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Self-replication is easy

Lets take the modular replicator in pieces and see what it takes
for it to make a copy of itself:

(1) Structure

Start with hexagonal bar stock.  Circular cut-off blade is attached
to end of module and driven by rotation joint.  Sure, it isn't that
fast, since a small robot will only have on the order of ten watts
to work with, but eventually you will cut off a six inch length.
Change to drill bit #1, and cut a hole lengthwize through the
stock.  Change to milling cutter #2 and cut slots though one
face and out the other side.  Rotate 60 degrees and repeat two
more times.  You now have a hexagonal frame that is hollow
inside to hold the other parts.

If you intend to have one face be a mirror, then use aluminum as
the starting material and simply not cut all the way through one
side.  This also allows you to leave some places to attach screws.

(2) Raw materials

A reducing furnace can be made in a vacuum chamber to reduce 
common oxides to metals and oxygen.  Any oxide will dissociate
to some extent at a high enough temperature.  If you have a
chamber with a temperature gradient, the raw material can be placed
at the hot end, and different metals will plate out from the vapor
at different locations, with the oxygen coming out last at the
coolest end.  Of course, the furnace itself must be made out
of refractory oxides.  The heat source is a lot of robot modules
collectively focussing their mirror sides on the furnace.

I would be happy to one or two metals (iron, magnesium, or aluminum),
or even a weird alloy combination of these (since getting pure
metals won't be easy with thermal plating out.  As long as the
material is reasonably machinable and reasonably conductive, we
can use it for structure and conductors.

Refractory oxides can be made by the simple technique of concentratin
g enough sunlight on a sample of rock.  Boil off everything else,
and what you have left will be a refractory.  If you can get
alumina in any reasonalby pure form, it can be both a refractory
and an abrasive (it's what grinding wheels and sandpaper use).

If we are going all the way to raw materials, the robots need to
see to search for ores, be able to measure the size and weight
and color of samples (size and color from the CCD, weight by how
much power to lift the rock).  It must also be able to dig, hence
the claw and shovel attachments.

(3) Motors

Requires wires and the ability to make gears and frames, then
winding the coils and assembling the motor.  More drilling and
milling for the frames, and coil winding can be done by wire
rolling onto a core rotated by a rotary joint.  You will need
fine grippers, which is more machining and milling to make.

(4) Electronics and photovoltaics

[TBD later]


