Subject: NANO:Critiques of Drexler

Nick Szabo asks why there has not been more debate/criticism/reaction about
Drexler's work in general and "Nanosystems" in particular:

>Andrew Hall:
>> Before I sit down and do some calculations and grind through this with
>> the effort needed to confirm or deny, I would like to see some expert
>> criticism of Drexler. 
>
>I'd like to see this, too.  I haven't even seen any reviews of
>_Nanosystems_ in a scientific journal; nor have I seen any 

I believe Ralph Merkle posted to sci.nanotech a review he saw in "Nature"
or "Science." Also, the special issue of "Science" that dealt with
nanotech-related issues (this was circa November 1991) had a page on
Drexler and his critics and had some critical remarks by a Stanford guy
(whose name I've forgotten).

(Drexler is scathing in his sarcasm directed at these critics...perhaps
word has spread about this. Personally, I wish he would tone down his
attacks on those who dare to raise questions about the glorious nanotech
future.)

>Drexler's fame and citation habits generated quite a bit of resentment
>with at least a few research scientists : the _Artificial Life I_ proceedings
>feature an article on the history of "nanotechnology" that covers
>everything from Heinlein's Waldo to Feynman to Ettinger to sub-micron 
>lithography, but dismisses Drexler with ill-concealed animosity.

The feud between Conrad Schneiker, the author of the paper Nick is
presumably referring to, "NanoTechnology with Feynman Machines: Scanning
Tunneling Engineering and Artificial Life," is perhaps not well known on
this list....especially since Conrad seems to have dropped off the radar
screen (at least I haven't heard about him lately). At the A-LIFE
conference, Drexler was highly suspicious of _me_ just because I spent some
time talking to Conrad! Drexler really had it in for him.

(The feud dates from around 1985-6, when something happened. Hard to say
who was "right." Certainly Schneiker's work is less impressive, and is even
flaky in some ways (Schneiker worked with Hameroff, of "cytoskeletal
consciousness" notoriety). Needless to say, Schneiker is not inside the
Drexler circle.) I though Schneiker's review of nanotechnology was
negligent and vindictive in the way it minimized Drexler's many
contributions.)

On why Drexler's book is not getting more academic attention (evidenced, by
Nick's arguments, by the lack of discussion, critical reviews, positive
reviews, etc.), I would assume there are several points to consider:

1. The various calculations are mostly physical chemistry and mechanical
engineering. Don't expect AI or space colonization experts to read and
critique it (though they may be inspired by it, may flip through it, etc.).

2. That there is little commercial development is a factor.

3. Drexler's confrontational arguing style is not conducive to debate...I
speak from *experience*! Eric *believes* so strongly in his vision of
general assemblers, life extension, cell repair, asteroid mining, etc. that
he finds it incomprehensible that the rest of us are not dropping what
we're doing and immediately beginning to work in this field.

I also think he believes most critics are either fools or knaves.

4. What's to debate, really? The ultimate vision is more a matter of
economic development paths, funding, etc. I liken the reaction to Drexler's
work to that with Babbage more than a century ago. Not the most original
comparison (many have made it), but one which increasingly rings true. To
wit:

- a good idea, many decades ahead of the supporting technology

- no plausible funding strategy (though some may argue in favor...fine, so
go out and get the funding from the private sector!)

- the most important issues, as with Babbage, cannot be addressed until
some stuff is actually built (programming and all the good stuff since 1950
didn't pick up until there were many kinds of machines and languages).


By locating himself in the "Foresight Institute" and surrounding himself
with True Believers, I think Eric is missing some major opportunities. He's
written three books already, and may be at work on a fourth. However, now
is the time for actual research (not that his calculations are not a form a
research...).

By contrast, I worked for Paul Hansma in 1972-3 when he first arrived at UC
Santa Barbara. He was setting up a lab to study Josephson junctions and
SQUIDS, and I volunteered to help--and it probably helped me get a job at
Intel in 1974, as I was the rare physics student who had some chip-building
experience.

What's happened to him since is truly astounding. He now has a dozen grad
students, is the world's leading applier of AFMs to biological systems, and
was in on the founding of Santa Barbara-based Digital Systems, makers of
the "NanoScope" STM/AFM. And Hansma's wife, now a prof in the biology
department, is probably the leading researcher in studying the sequencing
of DNA with Paul's gadgets.

In 1988, the "Crypto" conference was held, as always, at the UCSB campus. I
took Ralph Merkle on a tour of Hansma's labs, which now occupy most of the
top floor of the physics building.

(Pete Capello, if he reads this, is at UCSB and can certainly confirm what
I'm saying about Hansma's reputation. Hal Finney, who works in Santa
Barbara, may also know about this. And, yes, Drexler cites Hansma. I don't
know if the reverse is true...not saying this, understand.)

What I'm saying is that Eric needs to be involved in a major, multi-pronged
research effort. Why isn't he? Why isn't he at Stanford or MIT with a large
and growing team of grad students and postdocs? Leading research, teaching
some classes, and still writing some books. He now has a Ph.D., granted
recently, and so this is not a barrier, if it ever was.

To me, this is the more interesting question.


-Tim
